WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.960
HubSpot. Perhaps the most&nbsp;
important marketing company ever.&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.640
They invented inbound marketing and&nbsp;
reached a $42 billion market cap.&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:15.120
But, they might also be the weirdest company ever.&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:21.120
A candy wall, massage therapists, nerf gun&nbsp;
battles, bouncyball office chairs, a room packed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:28.240
with writers they called the "content factory.”
And now, their market cap has collapsed by 79%.&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.920
What happened to the most&nbsp;
important company in marketing?&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:50.160
Despite Hubspot still growing, and still making&nbsp;
money, everyone seems to be waiting for it to die.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:56.960
Do you notice how every company has blogs?
Kinda weird right? Why does, say, a shoe company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:02.240
have a blog section, and a newsletter?
That's largely because of HubSpot.&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:07.920
Before content marketing and social&nbsp;
media, marketing was more... abrupt.&nbsp;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.240
Worse? I’m not really sure…&nbsp;
but it sure was annoying.&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.800
Commercials during TV and movies, radio&nbsp;
advertisements, billboards dominated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:24.320
public spaces. Spam emails filled inboxes.
And the worst, Telemarketers. Calls during dinner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:29.200
during the day, marketing felt really&nbsp;
abrupt and something done “at” you.&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.160
Two MIT graduates, Brian Halligan and&nbsp;
Dharmesh Shah, noticed the same thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:39.040
but from the seller's side: buyers&nbsp;
had learned to tune everything out.&nbsp;

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:44.320
So in 2006, they built HubSpot to&nbsp;
help small businesses solve this.&nbsp;

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:49.360
What if marketing worked in reverse?
What if customers came looking for the company?

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.000
In the early 2000s, people started using search&nbsp;
engines and blogs to research products for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.160
themselves. You collect information on your&nbsp;
own terms. Blogs were a huge part of this.&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:05.120
HubSpot built a toolbox for turning strangers&nbsp;
on the internet into paying customers.&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:09.840
Their strategy is simple: first&nbsp;
publish content that answers questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:15.360
rank on Google with good SEO, convert&nbsp;
the readers into buyers, or even fans.&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.480
With this, they coined the&nbsp;
term "inbound marketing" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:24.000
wrote the literal book on it in 2010.
But that was just the beginning. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.040
built an entire toolbox for marketers.
One of its earliest tools was Website&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:34.560
Grader. Businesses could enter their website&nbsp;
and get a free report showing SEO issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:40.880
performance problems, and ways to improve. This&nbsp;
being free, back in the day, was unheard of.&nbsp;

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:46.640
Just between 2006 and 2011 it&nbsp;
graded more than 4 million websites&nbsp;

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:51.200
With Twitter grader they took it a step further,&nbsp;
customers shared their Twitter scores to their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.240
followers who became inspired to do the same,&nbsp;
generating tons of awareness for HubSpot.&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:02.080
The thing about HubSpot though… they practiced&nbsp;
what they preached, HubSpot's blog was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:08.480
company's growth engine, and the proof of concept.
Thousands and thousands of HubSpot blogs…&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:14.000
A blog could pull 100,000 visitors which can&nbsp;
retain 2,000 subscribers to 100 qualified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:20.240
leads. Even for a blog post written years ago.
Free tools and hundreds, then soon thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:25.200
of blogs… HubSpot became the&nbsp;
cultural center of online marketing.&nbsp;

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:29.440
But there’s one question.
If all this stuff is free…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:34.480
how did they make any money?
Ads? Well, surprisingly no.&nbsp;

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:39.200
The blogs and free tools were the content&nbsp;
marketing that brought customers into their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:47.040
paid services: CRM, marketing tools, sales tools,&nbsp;
and service "hubs" priced per seat and by tier.&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.720
It’s a simple idea you see with so many marketing&nbsp;
funnels for SEO companies or SaaS companies.&nbsp;

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:57.840
Give away a bit of real value for free,&nbsp;
draw the customer into the paid content.&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:03.680
And, it worked. Big time.
After launching in 2007, HubSpot generated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.880
$255,000 in revenue in just its first year.
Three years later, it had multiplied that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:14.320
figure by 61, reaching $15.6 million.
That kind of growth attracted major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:21.280
investors, including Sequoia Capital and Google
Ironically, years later, Google's own decisions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:26.640
would become HubSpot's biggest problems.
But for now, things were taking off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:30.800
and this was just the beginning.
After years of launching free products,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:38.400
HubSpot went public in 2014 at $25&nbsp;
per share, raising $133.7 million.&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.760
Most companies slow down after an IPO.&nbsp;
HubSpot’s system helped it expand more.&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:48.000
In 2016, HubSpot generated&nbsp;
$271 million in revenue.&nbsp;

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.400
Three years later, that figure had&nbsp;
more than doubled to $675 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:57.600
with over 73,000 customers.
Then COVID accelerated everything.&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:02.560
Companies that were late to the online game&nbsp;
suddenly had no choice. Which created a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:08.160
demand for digital marketing and sales tools.
HubSpot's customer base jumped 39% in a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:14.640
year, reaching nearly 96,000 customers.
By 2021, revenue had crossed $1.28 billion.&nbsp;

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:22.080
Then in 2024, revenue had doubled to $2.63&nbsp;
billion from almost 248,000 customers.&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.600
It’s kind of crazy how dominant HubSpot was.&nbsp;

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:30.640
Revenue grew more than 10,000-fold&nbsp;
from 2007 to 2024, while its customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:38.400
base expanded nearly 20 times after the IPO.
Growth rarely fell below 20% for years and years.&nbsp;

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:44.240
HubSpot was the gem of the marketing&nbsp;
world. But, it had two major problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:49.760
that didn’t “seem” like problems, on the inside.
The first actually relates to one of its first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:54.800
investors: Google. Who ironically would&nbsp;
become responsible for its downfall.&nbsp;

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:59.600
But second, was much stranger…&nbsp;
HubSpot’s very… weird culture.&nbsp;

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:05.600
On the outside, HubSpot was professional, the&nbsp;
marketing standard, if a bit quirky and fun.&nbsp;

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:14.720
Inside, was one of the weirdest&nbsp;
company cultures in history.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:25.520
Back in April 2013, Dan Lyons&nbsp;
started his first day at HubSpot.&nbsp;

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:29.760
A journalist at fifty years old,&nbsp;
with a wife and two young children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.800
he suddenly found himself unemployed.
HubSpot, a fast-growing tech startup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:40.880
had offered him a stack of stock options for a&nbsp;
vaguely defined role called "Marketing Fellow."&nbsp;

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:47.120
And, his experience was so strange and so wild,&nbsp;
he wrote an entire book about it: Disrupted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:52.000
which many of the following quotes come from.
This book would send HubSpot leadership into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.720
panic, even causing the FBI to get involved.
[Dan Lyons]: “I’ve spent the past decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:59.840
writing satire about the technology industry…but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:04.800
nothing i ever dreamed up in those fictional&nbsp;
accounts could compare to the ridiculousness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:10.960
I encountered when I took a job at an actual&nbsp;
tech company, a software maker called HubSpot”

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:15.280
[Dan Lyons]“The offices bear a striking&nbsp;
resemblance to the Montessori preschool that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:21.280
my kids attended: lots of bright basic colors,&nbsp;
plenty of toys, and a nap room with a hammock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:27.280
and soothing palm tree murals on the wall. The&nbsp;
office-as-playground trend started at Google but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:34.000
now has spread like an infection across the tech&nbsp;
industry. Work can't just be work; work has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:40.800
be fun. HubSpot is divided into "neighborhoods,"&nbsp;
each named after a section of Boston: North End,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:46.640
South End, Charlestown. One neighborhood has a&nbsp;
set of musical instruments, in case people want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:54.480
to have an impromptu jam session, which Zack says&nbsp;
never happens; the instruments just sit there.”&nbsp;

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:58.320
HubSpot embraced every tech&nbsp;
startup cliché imaginable.&nbsp;

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:03.280
A candy wall. A game room. Bouncy-ball&nbsp;
desk chairs. A noon push-up club in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:06.160
the lobby. Nerf gun wars.
[Dan Lyons] “Teams go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:10.960
outings to play trampoline dodgeball&nbsp;
and race go-karts and play laser tag”.&nbsp;

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:17.040
Odd, though some people like that kind of culture.
But, we’re just getting started.&nbsp;

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:22.640
Friday parties starting at 4:30 rage into the&nbsp;
night; "shower pods" that became hook-up dens.&nbsp;

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:27.120
[Dan Lyons]“I hear a story about janitors&nbsp;
coming in one Saturday morning to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:31.840
the following things in the first-floor&nbsp;
men's room: a bunch of half-empty beers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:39.040
a huge pool of vomit. The janitors were not happy.&nbsp;
They get even more distressed when, one morning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:44.320
a twenty-something guy from the HubSpot marketing&nbsp;
department arrives wasted and, for reasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:51.200
unknown, sets a janitor's cart on fire.
But maybe that’s just some bad actors.&nbsp;

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.840
Though, HubSpot’s approach&nbsp;
to everything, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.840 --> 00:09:00.080
content and their staff, is quite off-putting.
[Dan Lyons] “Everyone works in vast, open spaces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:05.840
crammed next to one another like seamstresses in&nbsp;
Bangladeshi shirt factories, only instead of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:11.200
hunched over sewing machines they are hunched over&nbsp;
laptops. Zack takes me to a cramped little shoebox&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:16.960
of a room, about fifteen feet wide and thirty feet&nbsp;
long, where twenty young women are packed into two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:23.600
rows, staring at laptops. This is the content&nbsp;
factory. That is literally what they call it.”&nbsp;

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:27.120
Things get even weirder.
A co-founder bringing a teddy bear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:31.360
to meetings to represent the customer.
All-nighters to churn out blog posts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:36.160
were branded as 'content hackathons.'
A "seating hack" policy, by which employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:39.520
swap seats every three months.
If an employee is fired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:44.880
the company disguises it as a "graduation".
[Dan Lyons]: “Zack points to an empty desk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:50.480
"I guess you can sit there," he says. Instead&nbsp;
of a chair, there is a big rubber ball—orange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:55.680
of course—on a rolling frame. I'm not&nbsp;
quite sure what to do. If I ask for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.680 --> 00:10:00.880
chair, I risk looking like an old fart who&nbsp;
doesn't know how to sit on a bouncy ball.”&nbsp;

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:07.200
Also didn’t help Lyons, that the CEO of HubSpot&nbsp;
Brian Halligan said “In the tech world, gray hair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:11.360
and experience are really overrated”
While Lyons, who very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:16.560
had gray hair, was working there.
I dunno if you guys have seen Parks and Rec,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:20.400
but this reminds me of the fictional telecom&nbsp;
company Gryzzl (Pronounced Grizz-ill), a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:26.960
parody of absurd startup culture. But&nbsp;
sometimes reality is crazier than fiction.&nbsp;

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:31.440
Some call this “lifestyle branding”, where&nbsp;
companies use culture and aesthetic as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:35.600
a substitute for traditional&nbsp;
compensation or job security.&nbsp;

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:39.760
This was the kind of company that called&nbsp;
the office a family, while underneath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:46.147
all that fun were young workers hanging on low&nbsp;
salaries, distracted by all the “fun” benefits.&nbsp;

00:10:46.147 --> 00:10:49.840
[Dan Lyons]: “Hubspotters were true&nbsp;
believers. They were making the world a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:55.520
better place... by selling email spam."
While all this was happening, HubSpot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:59.040
was simultaneously winning&nbsp;
"Best Place to Work" awards.&nbsp;

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:02.560
Am I saying no one liked&nbsp;
working there? Of course not.&nbsp;

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:06.000
But, it certainly is weird&nbsp;
looking back in retrospect.&nbsp;

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:11.760
One man's cult is another's dream job I guess.
Lyons quit after about two years and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:15.200
wrote his book: Disrupted.
Yet before it's even published,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:21.600
HubSpot finds out, and things turn pretty dark.
To HubSpot, this was a huge threat to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:25.200
its share price and its image.
Executives considered the book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:30.000
"a financial threat to HubSpot, its share&nbsp;
price, and the company’s future potential".&nbsp;

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.840
There were multiple attempts to obtain&nbsp;
the manuscript through email hacking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:38.880
extortion, and efforts to gather sensitive&nbsp;
information on people connected to the book.&nbsp;

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:45.600
These were “allegedly so aggressive—hacking and&nbsp;
extortion—that the FBI launched an investigation”&nbsp;

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:49.680
HubSpot then fired its chief&nbsp;
marketing officer, Mike Volpe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:54.800
after an internal investigation concluded that&nbsp;
he had violated the company's code of ethics.&nbsp;

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:59.120
HubSpot Vice President of Content&nbsp;
Joe Chernov also resigned.&nbsp;

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:03.680
According to the company, Volpe had been&nbsp;
involved in efforts to obtain an unpublished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:06.480
manuscript of the book.
Even Brian Halligan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:11.520
the founder and CEO was caught up in this.
He was sanctioned for knowing about the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:16.080
and failing to inform the board in time.
[Lyons]: "I didn't know if someone was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:20.720
breaking into my house or hacking my&nbsp;
computers. I didn't know what happened."&nbsp;

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:27.520
It got so bad that he filed a FOIA request with&nbsp;
the FBI to find out what had been done to him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:34.080
only to receive heavily redacted pages in return.
Ironically, parts of the book may have even helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:39.760
recruiting by portraying HubSpot as a place where&nbsp;
work was at least fun, because everyone already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:44.720
knew about the absurdity of startup culture.
But Dan Lyons would soon become the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:49.200
least of HubSpot's concerns.
Years later, a much bigger force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.200 --> 00:13:05.200
would disrupt HubSpot's entire business model.
One that invested in HubSpot many years ago.&nbsp;

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:09.840
The world of business, marketing,&nbsp;
and tech, is continuously changing.&nbsp;

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:14.640
Companies rise and fall all the&nbsp;
time, with some pretty crazy stories.&nbsp;

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:20.880
We cover these every week, so if you haven’t,&nbsp;
subscribe, so you don’t miss future videos.

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:28.160
In September 2021, HubSpot stock closed&nbsp;
at an all-time high of $852 per share.&nbsp;

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:36.640
From its $25 IPO price, that was a 34x return.&nbsp;
HubSpot was now a 40 billion dollar company.&nbsp;

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:41.120
It looked unstoppable, but&nbsp;
then the slow leak began.&nbsp;

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:47.360
In January 2023, HubSpot announced its first&nbsp;
layoffs in company history. On the same day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:50.960
HubSpot closed some office spaces&nbsp;
and concentrated employees into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:55.600
one main location to save money&nbsp;
and operate more efficiently.&nbsp;

00:13:55.600 --> 00:14:01.280
But the real problem wasn't inside&nbsp;
HubSpot, it was coming from Google.

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:07.040
In April 2024, Google explored a&nbsp;
$35 billion acquisition of HubSpot.&nbsp;

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:12.720
This would be Alphabet's largest acquisition&nbsp;
ever, with many reports of progress through May.&nbsp;

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:19.760
But then, on July 10, 2024, this fell through with&nbsp;
no explanation. This sent Hubspot’s stock down by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:25.120
19%, its worst since 2020.
Why did Google back out?&nbsp;

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:30.480
During this period, HubSpot was growing faster&nbsp;
than Google, reporting revenue growth of more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:37.520
20% for six consecutive quarters, while Alphabet&nbsp;
had not exceeded 20% growth since early 2022.&nbsp;

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:39.920
So why?
We don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:46.560
really know. But we have a pretty good guess.
At the time, Google was already fighting a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:51.040
battle over monopolizing search engine&nbsp;
and search advertising markets. (Show).&nbsp;

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:56.640
So, trying to also claim a huge part of CRM&nbsp;
software, the acquisition would have faced all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.640 --> 00:15:00.400
kinds of anti-trust blockades.
It just wasn’t worth the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:03.360
hassle. (Show bottom paragraph).
But, from here, things went from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:06.800
bad to worse for HubSpot.
Way worse.&nbsp;

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:12.880
In 2024, Google launched AI Overviews.
Traffic to many blogs fell off a cliff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:18.480
and HubSpot was among the most affected.
Users no longer clicked on blogs like HubSpot’s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:23.920
They simply asked AI for the answer and,&nbsp;
at most, glanced at the sources it cited.&nbsp;

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:28.640
HubSpot's monthly traffic fell from&nbsp;
13.5 million visits in November 2024&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:35.360
to 8.6 million in December alone. Nearly 5&nbsp;
million visits disappeared in a single month.&nbsp;

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:38.640
Zoom out further, and the&nbsp;
decline looks even worse.&nbsp;

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:46.080
In March 2023, HubSpot's blog attracted 24.4&nbsp;
million monthly visits. By January 2025,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:52.960
that figure had fallen to just 6.1 million.
In less than two years, as much as 75–80%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:57.440
of its traffic had vanished.
Before, a CRM company could rank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:02.160
for famous quotes, resignation letters, and&nbsp;
cover letter templates to generate traffic.&nbsp;

00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:06.960
But Google's AI-powered search is&nbsp;
increasingly rewarding expertise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:12.320
When a website tries to rank for everything,&nbsp;
it becomes harder for Google to determine what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:18.160
that site actually specializes in, making it&nbsp;
less likely to appear as a trusted source.&nbsp;

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:22.240
None of these had much to do with&nbsp;
the software it actually sold.&nbsp;

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:26.160
This flooded the algorithm.
The volume strategy, or rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:31.680
the “content factory,” was backfiring.
After being a content factory for almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:36.240
two decades, they quietly deleted&nbsp;
more than 30,000 blog posts.&nbsp;

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:41.120
They later admitted the strategy had&nbsp;
been showing cracks as far back as 2020.&nbsp;

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:43.440
And it gets even worse.
Resolution / Payoff&nbsp;

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:50.080
In 2026, the rise of AI raised doubts across&nbsp;
the software market, especially in SaaS.&nbsp;

00:16:50.080 --> 00:16:54.400
But HubSpot beat expectations&nbsp;
with its new AI integration.&nbsp;

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:59.920
Revenue grew 23% to $881 million,&nbsp;
customers approached 300,000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:05.840
and management even raised its forecast.
With those numbers, it unveiled its survival bet:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.840 --> 00:17:11.360
outcome-based AI pricing, where customers pay&nbsp;
only when an AI agent successfully resolves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:17.680
a ticket, alongside price cuts and free trials.
The market ignored HubSpot's long history of free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:24.240
tools and saw something else: desperation.
The stock fell 19% the next day.&nbsp;

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:30.240
Today, HubSpot is worth roughly $11–12 billion,&nbsp;
down from nearly $40 billion at its peak.&nbsp;

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:34.160
The strange thing is that&nbsp;
HubSpot hasn't collapsed.&nbsp;

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:40.160
Revenue is still growing. Reportedly, AI&nbsp;
agents are now resolving around 70% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:46.640
support tickets across 9000 activated customers.
Among AI-generated answers, HubSpot is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.640 --> 00:17:52.400
the most recommended brand in its category.
And, most importantly, they’re actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:56.640
making a profit now.
Yet despite all that, their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.640 --> 00:18:02.960
market cap is the lowest it’s been in 6 years.
The market is still judging what AI might do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:10.080
to every SaaS company, including Hubspot.
Even if Hubspot has pivoted… AI is hugely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:16.640
disrupting SEO, blogs, and seat-based&nbsp;
SAAS companies. Hubspot does all of these.&nbsp;

00:18:16.640 --> 00:18:21.360
Investors aren’t convinced HubSpot's&nbsp;
best days are still ahead of it.

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:26.000
It’s not just HubSpot though, the entire&nbsp;
world of SaaS seems to be collapsing.&nbsp;

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:31.280
Another case is Monday.com, who seemed&nbsp;
to have an ad on every YouTube video.&nbsp;

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:36.960
They spent more on marketing than they&nbsp;
had revenue, but now, they’re crumbling.&nbsp;

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:38.880
